power is being transformed in the uk  to get to net zero fossil fuels  largely consigned to history  the uk is the biggest importer in the  world of biomass or wood pellets  but in this critical year to stop the  clock on a climate disaster  burning wood for energy is facing fresh  scrutiny  along with the billion pounds the  government pays to subsidize it  trees play an important part in helping  to tackle climate change  they naturally soak up carbon but the  european union  is currently reviewing its policies  around the burning of wood to generate  energy  and there are calls for other countries  including the uk  to do the same jean pascal van impe  was vice chairman of the ipcc  the united nations body which assesses  the science on climate change  we are really creating a depth you know  a big carbon depth by burning  today that those pellets  that would in power plants because we  release the co2 now  hoping that future woods  will absorb co2 in the future  jean pascal is one of 500 scientists who  recently wrote to world leaders  calling for biomass not to be classed as  carbon neutral  they said we urge you not to undermine  both climate goals and the world's  biodiversity  by shifting from burning fossil fuels to  burning trees to generate energy  to meet future net zero emission goals  your governments should work to preserve  and restore forests  and not to burn them last year in the uk  biomass powered six and a half percent  of the national grid  data shared with sky news from the think  tank ember  calculated that two uk power stations  got just over a billion pounds in  subsidies  and were excused from paying carbon  taxes totaling over 300  million pounds to subsidize  um an activity that has  negative consequences for climate and  the environment  is is totally contradictory with the  goals of the paris agreement  and and the goals of the uh  the conference due to take place in  glasgow at the end of the year  drax power station in north yorkshire  sees itself as part of the climate  solution  not the problem and wants to be carbon  negative by 2030.  drax survived by switching from coal to  biomass  delivered by train almost all of its  biomass is imported  450 000 tons biomass pellet plant  owned and operated by drax in louisiana  nearly two-thirds of the wood pellets  used by drax come from forests in the  southern united states  drax says it's confident more carbon is  being stored than is being removed  whenever you remove wood from a forest  you have to account for this co2 that  you've removed  and that's the system which we work  under and the deity  so i'm aware there are cause for these  systems to be looked at do you accept in  any way  that the use of biomass is controversial  obviously i'm aware that some people  have concerns over biomass  what i would say is there's good biomass  and bad biomass  and you have to be really clear where  your biomasses come from  and be able to audit every part of that  supply chain  there's no dispute that the burning of  biomass emits more carbon than coal at  the smokestack  but as a renewable energy source the  emissions are not included in government  figures  if you did include emissions from the  new biomass we'd be making much slower  progress towards net zero than  than people tend to think so that means  having another look at the biomass the  policy framework surrounds the biomass  industry  the department of business energy and  industrial strategy  says subsidies are only given to biomass  which complies with strict  sustainability criteria and biomass is a  valuable part of the national grid  that may not satisfy environmentalists  who want the government to take  another look at the use of biomass in  this critical year for the climate  lisa holland sky news
 

